Zstd compression

Installation if not present
apt update && apt install -y zstd


To view the contents of the compressed files
zstdcat wp-debug-2026-05-01.log.zst | head -n 5


To search characters inside the compressed files
zstdgrep "PHP Fatal error" wp-debug-*.log.zst


Decompress a single file if needed:
unzstd wp-debug-2026-05-01.log.zst


Benchmark Without Saving to Disk (Fastest)
zstd -b3 /path/to/sample.log

The built-in benchmark flag (-b) reads the file, tests compression and decompression speeds in memory, and prints the exact compression ratio and throughput (MB/s).

 zstd -b3 2026-05-03.log
 3#2026-05-03.log    : 895608980 ->  54209642 (x16.52),  779.2 MB/s, 3022.1 MB/s


Alternatively, to test piped streaming:
# Check compression ratio via pipe without writing to disk
cat /path/to/sample.log | zstd -3 -c | wc -c


Test a Small Sample Chunk First
Extract a 1GB or 2GB slice of the file to inspect the real .zst file output:

# 1. Grab the first 2GB of the log
head -c 2G /path/to/sample.log > test_chunk.log

# 2. Compress with zstd
zstd -3 test_chunk.log -o test_chunk.log.zst

# 3. Compare sizes
ls -lh test_chunk.log test_chunk.log.zst


Side-by-Side Test (zstd vs gzip)
To see the difference in both time and compressed size on your specific hardware:


# Time and test gzip
time gzip -c test_chunk.log > test_chunk.log.gz

# Time and test zstd
time zstd -3 -c test_chunk.log > test_chunk.log.zst

# View size difference
ls -lh test_chunk.log*


1. Compress an Entire Directory
using Modern tar (Ubuntu 20.04+)
Modern versions of tar have built-in support for --zstd:
tar --zstd -cvf archive_name.tar.zst /path/to/directory

Pipe Method (Control CPU Threads & Compression Level)
If you want multi-threading or a specific compression level:

tar -cf - /path/to/directory | zstd -3 -T0 -o archive_name.tar.zst

-3: Compression level.

-T0: Uses all available CPU cores automatically.


2. Decompress / Extract the Archive

# Direct extraction
tar --zstd -xvf archive_name.tar.zst

# Or extract to a specific target folder
tar --zstd -xvf archive_name.tar.zst -C /path/to/destination/


3. List Contents Without Extracting

tar --zstd -tvf archive_name.tar.zst



How to View & Search Inside .tar.zst Archives
1. View a Specific File Inside the Archive
Extract the file directly to stdout (-O) without touching the disk:


# View first 10 lines of a specific file inside the tarball
tar --zstd -xOf archive.tar.zst path/to/file.log | head -n 10


2. Grep Across All Files Inside the Archive
Stream the uncompressed tar content through tar to stdout, then pipe to grep:

# Grep through the entire archive stream
tar --zstd -xOf archive.tar.zst | grep "PHP Fatal error"

Note: -O merges all file streams together, so standard grep won't tell you which specific filename matched.


3. Grep Inside a Specific File Inside the Archive
tar --zstd -xOf archive.tar.zst path/to/wp-debug-2026-05-01.log | grep "PHP Fatal error"

4. List File Names First

tar --zstd -tf archive.tar.zst | grep "2026-05"


# Test a Gzip archive for integrity errors
gzip -t archive.tar.gz

# Test a Zstd archive for integrity errors
zstd -t archive.tar.zst
